Jacek Dziedzic <jacek__NOSPAM__@janowo.net> wrote:

>my last wizard was
>generated with a ring of slow digestion 
>I'm satiated
>all the time -- very nice for my wizard. :)

No. With a ring of slow digestion, you can and should try to keep to a
level of not satiated and not hungry.

>   Now for my major problems with the wizard. First of all --
>I haven't had a chance to identify *any* of my items. I found
>a few potions, a few scrolls, two wands and three rings
>and I have no idea what each one represents. How do I go
>about identifying them? 

If you want to be thoroughly spoiled, read David Damerell's
identification spoiler. Price identification is a good start to get a
general appraisal of the items, and in some cases even a complete
identification of its kind.

>Do I need to carry the books? 

You will not need the books for a long time. Shops are a good place to
store them; when you need them back, you will have the offensive or
fincancial means to get them. Monsters do not use spellbooks.

>What about other potentially
>useful items (I found a leash, a whistle, a towel, a blindfold,
>a tin opener, etc.) 

A blindfold is very useful. A leash is useful only for pets. 

>If not, how do I store them safely in some place?

The first level of Sokoban is a convenient stash for items: When you
enter a level only to get to the stash, no wandering monsters will be
able to get at your potions etc.

>   Third of all -- how do I acquire lower AC as a wizard?
>I realize that most armour makes no sense for a wizard because
>it reduces spellcasting abilities. So... what kind of armour
>is 'safe' for a wizard? The only I've got besides my cloak
>of magic resistance is a cloak of displacement, or something
>like it, that hardly improves my AC, but confuses my opponents
>in a way. 

And you lose magic resistance, which is bad. Leathery kinds of armour and
helmets provide AC without reducing you spell casting abilities. Do not
let the failure rates in spells such as identify or stone to flesh
discourage you from getting better armour: To cast such spells, you can
always take off the helmet or iron shoes. Failure rates in combat spells
are much more critical.

